# ec/tools/grade_0751_isolation.py --self-test, wired into the cheap gate.
# Prepared here rather than landed, for the reason
# docs/ci/agent-gates-capture-claims.patch gives: `.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh`
# is copied from the agent-pipeline template and the pipeline's push token has no
# `workflow` scope, so a branch editing it fails at the end of the PR rather than
# at the start of it. `docs/agent-pipeline.md` carries this one across a re-copy
# too, as its item 7.
#
# To land it:
#
#   git apply docs/ci/agent-gates-0751-self-test.patch
#
# and that is the whole change.
#
# Why the cheap tier and not agent-gates-deep.sh: what the mode reads is the
# committed CSVs under `ec/tools/testdata/` and the standard library's
# `unittest`. No firmware image, no Ghidra, no network, no assembler -- so by the
# split `docs/agent-pipeline.md` records, it belongs beside
# `check_register_counts`, not in the deep tier. It measures 0.32 s end to end
# here (the 76 tests are 0.192-0.193 s of that, the rest interpreter start),
# against a cheap tier recorded at 5.9 s, on 2026-09-25. That is one runner's
# figure and the ratio is the point; `docs/agent-pipeline.md` item 6 gives the
# same caveat for `call_graph.py`.
#
# What it buys, and what it does not: the grader's 76 committed tests -- 72
# before this branch's two, plus #530's two that reached `main` meanwhile -- are
# the pinned record of a dozen refusal policies, and `grep -rn 'grade_0751'
# .github/` finds nothing, so a PR that breaks one merges green. **Until a
# human lands this, nothing runs the suite per commit, and a PR that breaks one
# of its refusals still merges green.** No line of that patch, of the mode, or
# of the write-up beside it is a claim about a laptop: the suite reads
# hand-built fixtures, and no EC is opened and no register read back.
#
# The comment it adds at the call site says the same thing where the next reader
# of the gate will see it, rather than only here.

diff --git a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
--- a/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
+++ b/.github/scripts/agent-gates.sh
@@ -123,6 +123,7 @@
               ec/tools/grade_name_basis.py \
               ec/tools/group_functions.py \
               ec/tools/call_graph.py \
+              ec/tools/grade_0751_isolation.py \
               bios/tools/bios_extract.py \
               windows/tools/decompile_native.py; do
     [ -f "$tool" ] || continue
@@ -187,6 +188,27 @@
       *call_graph.py)
         python3 "$tool" --check && python3 "$tool" --self-test || rc=1
         ;;
+      # The 0x0751 isolation grader's refusals, and the one tool here that runs
+      # its own unittest suite rather than a hand-written list of known
+      # answers: grade_0751_isolation.py --self-test discovers
+      # test_grade_0751_isolation.py in that tool's own directory and runs it in
+      # a subprocess, which is what keeps two copies of one file out of one
+      # interpreter. The tool takes a required capture positional and has
+      # neither --check nor a --work dir, so --self-test alone is the whole of
+      # it here -- the *) default below would hand it flags it does not have.
+      # What it holds is a dozen refusal policies, each of which is a gate
+      # between a human's hardware day and a wrong §7 call: a mark set that
+      # does not hold, a void block, a withheld window, a --block that named no
+      # block, a capture given twice, a mark no block can be attributed to. A
+      # PR that breaks one of them merges green today. Cheap tier because of
+      # what it reads: committed CSVs under ec/tools/testdata/ and the standard
+      # library's unittest -- no firmware image, no Ghidra, no network, no
+      # assembler. It prints its own deferral, that none of it is hardware
+      # evidence, and it asserts no test count: the exit code decides, for the
+      # reason tools/run-tests.sh gives.
+      *grade_0751_isolation.py)
+        python3 "$tool" --self-test || rc=1
+        ;;
       *)
         # build_ec_decompile.py and bios_extract.py both take --work.
         python3 "$tool" --work "$scratch" --check && \
